---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { repository } from '../site';
---
<Site title="About Ruphish" description="What Ruphish is, the principles behind it, who maintains it, and how to get involved in the open-source successor to Gophish.">
  <article class="about shell">
    <header class="about-heading"><h1>Phishing simulations,<br />built in the open.</h1><p class="lead">Ruphish is an open-source, self-hosted phishing simulation platform. It continues the idea of Gophish on a new foundation and is maintained independently.</p></header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What Ruphish is</h2>
      <p>Ruphish helps teams prepare phishing simulations, send them through their own mail service, and understand how people respond. It runs as a standalone native executable or with Docker Compose and stores its data in SQLite, MySQL, or PostgreSQL.</p>
      <p>The source is available on <a href={repository}>GitHub</a> under the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT License</a>.</p>
      <h2>What guides the project</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Your installation, your data.</strong> Ruphish is self-hosted. Campaign content, recipients, and results stay in the database you operate.</li>
        <li><strong>Observe behaviour, not secrets.</strong> A simulation should show how people respond without putting them at risk. The built-in submission collector records interactions and whether a password was entered, never the entered values. The <a href="/docs/user-guide/results/participant-data/">participant-data guide</a> explains the model and its limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Results that say what they mean.</strong> Ruphish separates observed events from summary metrics and documents <a href="/docs/user-guide/results/understand-the-metrics/">what each metric can tell you</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Learning over blame.</strong> Learning pages explain the exercise right after a submission, so the simulation helps people recognise phishing next time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ruphish is meant for simulations you are authorised to run in your organisation.</p>
      <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
      <p>Ruphish is built and maintained by Simon Peter Rothgang, an Information Systems master’s student from Münster, Germany. It started with using Gophish during a master’s thesis on phishing and spear-phishing; <a href="/blog/introducing-ruphish/">Introducing Ruphish</a> tells that story.</p>
      <h2>Relationship to Gophish</h2>
      <p><a href="https://getgophish.com/">Gophish</a> made phishing simulation accessible to many people. Ruphish acknowledges that foundation and preserves attribution for reused material in its <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`}>third-party notices</a>. It is an independent project, without an official affiliation with the Gophish maintainers.</p>
      <p>Ruphish has its own interface and API. Supported Gophish SQLite and MySQL databases can be migrated; the <a href="/docs/migration/">migration guide</a> explains what to review first.</p>
      <h2>Where the project stands</h2>
      <p>Version 0.1.0 is the first release. The <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a> records what each release includes, and the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/ROADMAP.md`}>roadmap</a> describes the priorities for the next ones.</p>
      <h2>Get involved</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={`${repository}/issues`}>Report a bug or share a feature idea</a> on GitHub.</li>
        <li>Improve code, documentation, or translations by following the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>contributing guide</a>.</li>
        <li>Report suspected vulnerabilities privately as described in the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Participation follows the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`}>code of conduct</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Site>
